<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">Part 1: Warm-up =
&amp; Introduction (15 minutes)</h2>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Go=
od morning! I'm Sarah Chen, Principal Frontend Engineer here at NAB. I've b=
een with the bank for about 8 years, currently leading our digital banking =
platform's frontend architecture. Thanks for taking the time to interview w=
ith us today. Could you start by telling me a bit about yourself and your r=
ecent experience with performance optimization?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Good=
 morning Sarah, thank you for having me. I'm Alex, and I've been working as=
 a Senior Frontend Engineer for the past 6 years, with my most recent 3 yea=
rs focused heavily on large-scale React applications. At my current company=
, I lead the performance optimization initiatives for our e-commerce platfo=
rm that serves about 2 million daily active users.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">I've been particularly focused o=
n <strong>Core Web Vitals optimization</strong> - we managed to improve our=
 LCP from 4.2 seconds to 1.8 seconds over the past year, which directly cor=
related with a 23% increase in conversion rates. My approach typically invo=
lves a combination of <strong>bundle optimization, critical resource priori=
tization, and progressive loading strategies</strong>. I'm also quite passi=
onate about performance monitoring - I've implemented comprehensive perform=
ance tracking using both synthetic and real user monitoring.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's impressive results on the LCP improvement. At NAB, we're dealing with =
similar scale challenges, especially with our Internet Banking platform. Be=
fore we dive deeper, I'm curious about your experience with the broader fro=
ntend ecosystem. What's your preferred state management approach for comple=
x applications, and how does performance factor into that decision?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Grea=
t question. My state management choice really depends on the application's =
complexity and performance requirements. For large-scale applications like =
banking platforms, I typically gravitate towards <strong>Zustand or Jotai f=
or granular state management</strong>, especially when performance is criti=
cal.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Here's my reasoning: <strong>Zus=
tand offers excellent bundle size</strong> (2.9KB vs Redux's ~10KB with too=
lkit), and its selector-based updates prevent unnecessary re-renders. For N=
AB's use case, this is crucial because financial data updates frequently, a=
nd you don't want the entire component tree re-rendering when just account =
balances change.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Jotai takes it further w=
ith atomic state management</strong> - perfect for scenarios like trading p=
latforms where you have hundreds of stocks updating independently. Each ato=
m can update without affecting others, giving you surgical precision in re-=
renders.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">However, I still use <strong>Red=
ux with RTK Query for complex banking workflows</strong> where you need pre=
dictable state transitions and extensive middleware. The key is using <code=
 class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000=
 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">reselect</c=
ode> heavily and implementing proper normalization. I've seen Redux apps wh=
ere poor selector design caused 10x performance degradation.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">For performance monitoring, I in=
strument all state updates with custom performance marks: <code class=3D"bg=
-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-=
pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">performance.mark('accou=
nt-balance-update-start')</code> to track state update latency in productio=
n.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: I =
like that you're thinking about instrumentation from the start. That's exac=
tly the mindset we need here. Let me ask you this - when you mention "surgi=
cal precision in re-renders," can you walk me through how you'd actually me=
asure and optimize render performance in a complex React application?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Abso=
lutely. Render performance optimization is a multi-layered approach. Let me=
 break down my methodology:</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>1. Measurement Foundatio=
n:</strong>
First, I implement comprehensive render tracking. I use React's Profiler AP=
I combined with custom hooks:</p>
